 ## Background
-The extension of NeRF to dynamic scenes emerged to address the "ghosting" artifacts inherent in static models when applied to video data [S1]. Early approaches treated time as an additional input dimension to the MLP, serving as a baseline for more complex deformation-based methods [S1]. Subsequent developments introduced canonical space warping, particle systems, and sparse latent representations to better handle the many-to-one mapping ambiguities and computational costs of 4D reconstruction [S1]. These methods are primarily used in 3D perception, dynamic portrait reconstruction, and novel view synthesis for video [S1].
+Dynamic NeRF research covers applications including indoor 3D reconstruction, portrait reconstruction, human rendering, editable interactive NeRF, and 3D perception [S1]. NeRF-MS (2023) and DP-NeRF (2023) address visual vagueness and motion blur in dynamic sequences using ternary losses and physical blur kernels [S1]. ER-NeRF (2023) and AvatarMAV (2023) focus on high-fidelity dynamic portrait and head avatar creation using conditional frameworks and motion-aware voxels [S1]. DCT-NeRF and DyBluRF utilize Discrete Cosine Transform parameterizations for smooth trajectory modeling and motion blur simulation [S2, unofficial].
 
